A resume after a flush continues the stream's timeline.

The rtptime stands still while the player is paused, but the device's
clock does not, and the device keeps the mapping between the two it
was given at the start. Under that mapping the first packets after a
resume, stamped with the old rtptime, are due in the past. The sync
packet sent just before them establishes a new mapping, but a device
that reads a packet before the sync plays it at once: a moment of the
song, then silence until the packets are due under the new mapping,
then the resume proper. Heard as a very brief piece of the song at
the press, now and then.

Now a flush marks the master session, and the first write after it
moves the rtptime on by the time that passed, so the old mapping and
the new one agree and the packets are due in the future whichever the
device uses. The gap in rtptime is harmless: the flush already told
the device to drop everything before it.

Applies on top of owntone-airplay-flush-drops-partial-packet.patch.

